<%- include('partials/header') %>
  <main>
    <section>
      <h2>Regression on Real-World Data</h2>
      <p>The earlier pages used a tiny five-point example. This page fits a regression to live data for nearly every country in the world. The server requests two indicators from the <a href="https://datahelpdesk.worldbank.org/knowledgebase/articles/889392">World Bank Indicators API</a>: <strong>GDP per capita</strong> and <strong>life expectancy at birth</strong>. It then fits the line with the <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/simple-statistics"><code>simple-statistics</code></a> Node package.</p>
      <p>Income is skewed, with a few very rich countries, so the input is the <em>logarithm</em> of GDP per capita. On a log scale, each step on the x-axis means income is ten times higher. The relationship is close to a straight line on this scale, which is the <a href="/assumptions">linearity assumption</a> at work.</p>
    </section>

<% if (error) { %>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Data Unavailable</h2>
      <p>The World Bank API could not be reached right now (<%= error %>). Please try again in a moment.</p>
    </section>
<% } else { %>
    <figure>
      <svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 <%= world.chart.width %> <%= world.chart.height %>" width="<%= world.chart.width %>" height="<%= world.chart.height %>" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of life expectancy against GDP per capita with a fitted regression line rising from left to right">
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="#0B1A2E"/>
<% world.chart.yTicks.forEach(t => { %>
        <line x1="<%= world.chart.pad %>" x2="<%= world.chart.width - world.chart.pad %>" y1="<%= t.pos %>" y2="<%= t.pos %>" stroke="#333333"/>
        <text x="<%= world.chart.pad - 8 %>" y="<%= t.pos + 4 %>" text-anchor="end"><%= t.label %></text>
<% }); %>
<% world.chart.xTicks.forEach(t => { %>
        <text x="<%= t.pos %>" y="<%= world.chart.height - world.chart.pad + 18 %>" text-anchor="middle"><%= t.label %></text>
<% }); %>
<% world.chart.points.forEach(p => { %>
        <circle cx="<%= p.x.toFixed(1) %>" cy="<%= p.y.toFixed(1) %>" r="3.5" fill="#66CCFF" fill-opacity="0.75"><title><%= p.name %></title></circle>
<% }); %>
        <line x1="<%= world.chart.line.x1 %>" y1="<%= world.chart.line.y1 %>" x2="<%= world.chart.line.x2 %>" y2="<%= world.chart.line.y2 %>" stroke="#FFA500" stroke-width="3"/>
        <text x="<%= world.chart.width / 2 %>" y="<%= world.chart.height - 10 %>" text-anchor="middle">GDP per capita (US$, log scale)</text>
        <text x="16" y="<%= world.chart.height / 2 %>" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 16 <%= world.chart.height / 2 %>)">Life expectancy (years)</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption><%= world.count %> countries in <%= world.year %>. Hover over a point to see the country.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>The Fitted Line</h2>
      <p class="formula">life expectancy = <%= world.intercept.toFixed(2) %> + <%= world.slope.toFixed(2) %> × log<sub>10</sub>(GDP per capita)</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Statistic</th><th>Value</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Slope</td><td><%= world.slope.toFixed(2) %> years</td><td>Gain in life expectancy for each tenfold increase in income</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Intercept</td><td><%= world.intercept.toFixed(2) %> years</td><td>Predicted value when log<sub>10</sub>(GDP) = 0, i.e. $1 per person (an extrapolation)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R<sup>2</sup></td><td><%= world.r2.toFixed(3) %></td><td>Share of the variation in life expectancy that income explains</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Correlation (r)</td><td><%= world.correlation.toFixed(3) %></td><td>Strength and direction of the linear relationship</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Median GDP per capita</td><td>$<%= Math.round(world.medianGdp).toLocaleString('en-US') %></td><td>Typical country income</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mean life expectancy</td><td><%= world.meanLife.toFixed(1) %> years</td><td>Average across countries (not weighted by population)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <div class="two-col">
      <section class="card">
        <h2>Above the Line</h2>
        <p>Countries with the largest positive residuals, where people live longer than income alone predicts.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Actual</th><th>Predicted</th><th>Residual</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
<% world.above.forEach(c => { %>
            <tr><td><%= c.name %></td><td><%= c.life.toFixed(1) %></td><td><%= c.predicted.toFixed(1) %></td><td>+<%= c.residual.toFixed(1) %></td></tr>
<% }); %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h2>Below the Line</h2>
        <p>Countries with the largest negative residuals, where life expectancy falls short of what income predicts.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Actual</th><th>Predicted</th><th>Residual</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
<% world.below.forEach(c => { %>
            <tr><td><%= c.name %></td><td><%= c.life.toFixed(1) %></td><td><%= c.predicted.toFixed(1) %></td><td><%= c.residual.toFixed(1) %></td></tr>
<% }); %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Reading the Result</h2>
      <p>The line shows a strong association: richer countries tend to have longer lives. It does not show that income <em>causes</em> longer life. Health spending, education and clean water all rise with income, and the regression cannot separate their effects. The residual tables are often the most interesting part. They point to countries whose outcomes differ from what income alone would suggest.</p>
    </section>
<% } %>

    <section class="sources">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.CD">World Bank: GDP per capita (current US$), NY.GDP.PCAP.CD</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.DYN.LE00.IN">World Bank: Life expectancy at birth, SP.DYN.LE00.IN</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://simple-statistics.github.io/">simple-statistics documentation</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
